coder › Implemented the expiring LRU `Cache` on branch `work/main` in two commits.

What changed:
- `src/cache.ts` — implemented `Cache<K,V>` backed by a `Map` in LRU→MRU insertion order:
  - Constructor throws `RangeError` when `!(capacity >= 1)` (covers `0`, negatives, and `NaN`).
  - `get` returns `undefined` and drops the entry if missing/expired; otherwise re-inserts it to mark it most-recently-used.
  - `set` stamps `now()`, replaces a live key in place (no eviction, refreshes value/time, marks MRU); an expired existing key is dropped as absent; otherwise prunes expired entries, then evicts the first (least-recently-used) key if at capacity before inserting.
  - `has`/`delete` return booleans like `Map`, treating expired entries as absent (dropping them, returning `false`) and not affecting recency.
  - `size` prunes expired entries against `now()` before counting.
  - Expiry test is `now - setAt >= ttlMs` (entry live for `ttlMs`, expired at exactly `ttlMs`).
- `src/cache.test.ts` — kept the existing test, added 13 tests: RangeError, missing key, TTL boundary, expired-as-absent for `has`/`delete`/`size`, size with partial expiry, LRU eviction, `get`-as-use, `has`-not-a-use, replace-without-evict + TTL reset, re-set marks MRU, re-set of expired key doesn't evict live entries, `delete`/`has` Map semantics.

Decisions not in the brief:
- Expiry boundary is `>= ttlMs` (standard TTL semantics).
- `set` prunes expired entries before the capacity check, so dead entries never force an eviction; if still full, the LRU live entry is evicted.
- `has` does not affect recency (only a successful `get` counts as a use).

